We're going to be going over some of the basics of PyTorch

6
00:00:17,497 --> 00:00:20,800
today, and you guys are going to be using PyTorch many times

7
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,240
with all of your psets.

8
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:24,760
And I think if you keep doing deep learning in the future,

9
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:26,240
everyone's using PyTorch.

10
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:28,560
And so lots of projects, research projects

11
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:32,920
and real industry projects, are actually using PyTorch.

12
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:36,360
So there's a few things to know about PyTorch.

13
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:41,340
And a few of the more important things are that PyTorch is fast.

14
00:00:41,340 --> 00:00:43,520
So we're able to run PyTorch on GPU.

15
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:46,640
And this allows us to actually complete powerful deep learning

16
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:50,800
models by actually running these things within a reasonable time

17
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:51,640
frame.

18
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:53,960
So GPUs really speed up the ability

19
00:00:53,960 --> 00:00:56,400
to perform operations that we use

20
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:58,560
within these machine learning models,

21
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:00,432
and PyTorch supports that ability.

22
00:01:00,432 --> 00:01:02,140
So you may have used something like NumPy

23
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:08,260
before, where PyTorch and NumPy have similar data operations,

24
00:01:08,260 --> 00:01:11,780
but we're able to use GPU and speed up within PyTorch.

25
00:01:11,780 --> 00:01:14,820
The other thing about PyTorch is it supports autograd.

26
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:18,820
So we talked about gradients a little bit in lecture today.

27
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:20,660
But autograd is basically the idea

28
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:23,460
that we can compute the gradients automatically

29
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:26,260
as we iterate through our models.

30
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:28,380
And so PyTorch makes this very easy for us.

31
00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:30,540
It makes it very intuitive and simple

32
00:01:30,540 --> 00:01:32,580
for us to actually train and update

33
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:35,580
the weights of our models.

34
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:38,140
So that's a few reasons of why we use PyTorch.

35
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:41,420
It's a great library, and I think you guys will hopefully

36
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:43,140
enjoy it.

37
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:45,980
So today, just a quick overview of what we're going to do,

38
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:49,500
I'm going to go over some of intro stuff of just how we use

39
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:52,340
PyTorch tensors and some of the simple operations

40
00:01:52,340 --> 00:01:53,542
on these tensors.

41
00:01:53,542 --> 00:01:55,500
And then we're going to go through a little bit

42
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:57,500
about autograd, and then a little bit

43
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:00,380
about how we can train a model.

44
00:02:00,380 --> 00:02:03,400
You'll go into a lot more depth on some of the psets,

45
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:06,040
but overall, this should give you a good start

46
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:09,188
to be able to understand how it actually works behind the scenes

47
00:02:09,188 --> 00:02:11,480
and how you're going to actually be able to use it well

48
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:13,440
on your psets.

49
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:16,660
So just to start off, just how many people have used PyTorch?

50
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:20,560
Maybe you could raise your hand if you've used PyTorch.

51
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:25,120
OK, so we definitely have a lot of newbies to PyTorch, I guess.

52
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:26,340
And that's totally fine.

53
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:28,173
I think you guys will all be able to pick it

54
00:02:28,173 --> 00:02:33,320
up and be able to be totally fine throughout the course.

55
