It's probably one of the latest posts.

60
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:45,920
And yeah, we'll get started.

61
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:49,280
So the first thing we have to do is we have to do a few imports.

62
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:53,912
PyTorch can be imported by importing torch here.

63
00:02:53,912 --> 00:02:56,120
And then we'll import a few other things as well just

64
00:02:56,120 --> 00:03:01,360
to help with this visualizing and creating tensors.

65
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:07,850
So the first topic we're going to go over is PyTorch tensors.

66
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:10,100
And PyTorch tensors is basically where we can actually

67
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:12,300
store data within PyTorch.

68
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:14,260
So if you ever had some input data

69
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:17,220
that you wanted to put into a machine learning model,

70
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:19,400
or if you wanted to compare some outputs,

71
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:22,600
these outputs are going to be represented as PyTorch tensors.

72
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:24,100
And so the first thing we'll go over

73
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:26,933
is how we can actually initialize a tensor.

74
00:03:26,933 --> 00:03:29,600
So the first way we can do this here is just directly from data.

75
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:31,200
You're probably not going to actually do this too often.

76
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:33,940
But it's good to know all the different ways that you can do.

77
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:37,020
So if we just have a Python list here

78
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:39,500
where we have the inputs 1, 2, 3, and 4,

79
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:42,620
we can create a tensor directly from that data.

80
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:46,620
So if we run that cell, we see that we create a tensor object

81
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:50,780
here, which is the same structure as that nested list

82
00:03:50,780 --> 00:03:51,840
that we saw before.

83
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:56,540
The next way we can do this is we

84
00:03:56,540 --> 00:04:00,880
can also transform and create tensors from a NumPy array.

85
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:03,280
So do we have a question back there?

86
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:04,323
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

87
00:04:04,323 --> 00:04:05,740
JAMISON MEINDL: Oh, you can't see?

88
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:12,400
Is that a little better?

89
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:15,040
One more?

90
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:17,399
Good?

91
00:04:17,399 --> 00:04:20,160
All right, so yeah, we can create tensors

92
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:22,000
from NumPy arrays.

93
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,240
NumPy arrays are obviously another common way

94
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:26,240
we can store data in Python.

95
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:28,240
And tensors and NumPy arrays store

96
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:30,240
a lot of the same structure.

97
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:32,280
So we can copy directly from NumPy arrays.

98
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:35,040
And it actually can use the same memory

99
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:38,160
in the system for both the tensor and NumPy array.

100
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:41,440
So if we do the same thing here, we're copying the same data.

101
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:46,580
And we can create a tensor with this torch.from_numpy function.

102
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:51,400
The next way we can create tensors

103
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:54,960
is if we wanted to create a tensor with a specific shape

104
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:56,960
and specific values within it, we

105
00:04:56,960 --> 00:04:59,880
might want to create a tensor of all ones or a tensor

106
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:05,110
with random numbers to initialize some of model.

107
00:05:05,110 --> 00:05:07,690
And what we can do here is just use this ones_like

108
00:05:07,690 --> 00:05:10,550
if we wanted to make a tensor of the same shape

109
00:05:10,550 --> 00:05:12,142
as another object.

110
00:05:12,142 --> 00:05:13,350
But there's also operations--

111
00:05:13,350 --> 00:05:15,270
I think I have that-- that's next, where

112
00:05:15,270 --> 00:05:17,270
you can input a specific shape.

113
00:05:17,270 --> 00:05:22,070
And then rand_like will give you a random number between 0 and 1

114
00:05:22,070 --> 00:05:23,090
in each element.

115
00:05:27,310 --> 00:05:30,770
Moving on, yeah, we can create tensors of specific shapes.

116
00:05:30,770 --> 00:05:34,150
Here, I'm using rand, ones, zeros, and you can also--

117
00:05:34,150 --> 00:05:36,910
there's more that you can find in the documentation

118
00:05:36,910 --> 00:05:39,710
of different types of initializations of each element

119
00:05:39,710 --> 00:05:41,310
that you can do.

120
00:05:41,310 --> 00:05:44,153
So yeah, we can also define tensors with a specific shape.

121
00:05:44,153 --> 00:05:46,070
So if you didn't already have a tensor defined

122
00:05:46,070 --> 00:05:47,730
that you were interested in the shape,

123
00:05:47,730 --> 00:05:52,390
you could, say, create a tensor of size 2 by 3.

124
00:05:52,390 --> 00:05:54,390
And we can do that in a few different ways here.

125
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:55,370
We can do random.

126
00:05:55,370 --> 00:05:57,150
We can do ones, zeros.

127
00:05:57,150 --> 00:05:58,790
There's a few other options.

128
00:05:58,790 --> 00:06:01,450
There's a few attributes of a tensor that are good to know.

129
00:06:01,450 --> 00:06:04,250
So the first one that we've seen already is the shape.

130
00:06:04,250 --> 00:06:07,010
So if we make a random tensor of size 3 by 4,

131
00:06:07,010 --> 00:06:10,530
we can get the shape by doing .shape.

132
00:06:10,530 --> 00:06:12,430
The next thing to know is the data type.

133
00:06:12,430 --> 00:06:15,850
So most of the tensors you're probably going to be using

134
00:06:15,850 --> 00:06:18,770
and that we use as inputs to machine learning models

135
00:06:18,770 --> 00:06:20,190
are going to be float32.

136
00:06:20,190 --> 00:06:23,970
So that's a 32-bit float.

137
00:06:23,970 --> 00:06:28,330
But you might see integers as well in some situations.

138
00:06:28,330 --> 00:06:32,490
But the float 32 is generally used because it's the fastest.

139
00:06:32,490 --> 00:06:37,050
And GPU hardware is optimized to use that.

140
00:06:37,050 --> 00:06:39,370
The last thing you see here is the device.

141
00:06:39,370 --> 00:06:43,970
We get the device the tensor is stored on by doing .device.

142
00:06:43,970 --> 00:06:47,370
And this is important if you're using both CPU and GPU.

143
00:06:47,370 --> 00:06:49,250
So we can only do operations between tensors

144
00:06:49,250 --> 00:06:50,545
that are on the same device.

145
00:06:50,545 --> 00:06:53,170
So if you have one tensor that's on a CPU and one tensor that's

146
00:06:53,170 --> 00:06:54,610
on a GPU, you won't be able to do

147
00:06:54,610 --> 00:06:56,110
any operations between the two.

148
00:06:56,110 --> 00:06:58,210
You'd have to move one to the other device

149
00:06:58,210 --> 00:07:00,870
before you can actually do any operations between them.

150
00:07:00,870 --> 00:07:04,550
And so you will probably be frequently

151
00:07:04,550 --> 00:07:07,370
be finding out which device your tensors are on and then moving

152
00:07:07,370 --> 00:07:08,870
them to the device you actually want

153
00:07:08,870 --> 00:07:10,550
them to be on if you wanted to do

154
00:07:10,550 --> 00:07:12,170
some operation between the two.

155
00:07:15,643 --> 00:07:17,310
The next thing we're going to talk about

156
00:07:17,310 --> 00:07:18,990
is just some of the basic operations

157
00:07:18,990 --> 00:07:21,150
that we can perform on tensors.

158
00:07:21,150 --> 00:07:24,110
So we can do things like, to modify a tensor

159
00:07:24,110 --> 00:07:28,590
or create a new tensor based on the tensor input,

160
00:07:28,590 --> 00:07:32,310
and these are pretty simple operations that include things

161
00:07:32,310 --> 00:07:34,910
like adding tensors together or multiplying them

162
00:07:34,910 --> 00:07:38,470
that we can do to get different data that we want.

163
00:07:38,470 --> 00:07:41,550
So if we look at an example, the first thing we'll do

164
00:07:41,550 --> 00:07:44,950
is let's just create some of input tensor.

165
00:07:44,950 --> 00:07:47,350
And this notation here is basically

166
00:07:47,350 --> 00:07:51,230
going to create a tensor of 101 equally-spaced numbers

167
00:07:51,230 --> 00:07:52,925
from 0 to 5.

168
00:07:52,925 --> 00:07:54,550
And so that might be something if we're

169
00:07:54,550 --> 00:07:59,150
interested in seeing the effect along a specific range

170
00:07:59,150 --> 00:08:02,090
in this case 0 to 5.

171
00:08:02,090 --> 00:08:04,070
And if we wanted to see that worked,

172
00:08:04,070 --> 00:08:05,930
we can print the first five things

173
00:08:05,930 --> 00:08:08,330
in x by just indexing in the same way

174
00:08:08,330 --> 00:08:14,450
we would in NumPy or other Python environments.

175
00:08:14,450 --> 00:08:18,370
So we can also get the last ones by doing negative 5

176
00:08:18,370 --> 00:08:24,050
to the end, which you may have seen in other Python libraries.

177
00:08:24,050 --> 00:08:27,730
So let's try and perform some operations on this tensor.

178
00:08:27,730 --> 00:08:31,490
So if we wanted to, say, graph a sine wave or cosine,

179
00:08:31,490 --> 00:08:34,289
we could do x.sin.

180
00:08:34,289 --> 00:08:38,770
And that will perform sine on each input in the tensor.

181
00:08:38,770 --> 00:08:40,809
We can also do something more complex

182
00:08:40,809 --> 00:08:45,730
like doing x to the power of x cosine to create

183
00:08:45,730 --> 00:08:49,530
a more interesting output.

184
00:08:49,530 --> 00:08:50,810
We can also subtract tensors.

185
00:08:50,810 --> 00:08:53,010
So we can subtract those two outputs

186
00:08:53,010 --> 00:08:56,510
or just find the specific value within that tensor.

187
00:08:56,510 --> 00:08:58,010
So if we wanted to find the min, you

188
00:08:58,010 --> 00:09:00,450
could also find max, mean, standard deviation,

189
00:09:00,450 --> 00:09:01,830
stuff like that.

190
00:09:01,830 --> 00:09:06,350
If you wanted to find some characteristic about a tensor,

191
00:09:06,350 --> 00:09:08,370
we can also use tensors with matplotlib.

192
00:09:08,370 --> 00:09:11,870
So maybe some of you have used matplotlib in other cases.

193
00:09:11,870 --> 00:09:14,910
And we can directly input these tensors

194
00:09:14,910 --> 00:09:17,630
and create plots with matplotlib.

195
00:09:17,630 --> 00:09:20,190
So we see here that some of our transformations

196
00:09:20,190 --> 00:09:23,470
of x from 0 to 5, and we can see how

197
00:09:23,470 --> 00:09:27,345
those different transformations occurred.

198
00:09:27,345 --> 00:09:29,470
So there's a lot of different operations we can do.

199
00:09:29,470 --> 00:09:31,830
I'm obviously not going to be able to go through all

200
00:09:31,830 --> 00:09:33,670
the different operations, but just

201
00:09:33,670 --> 00:09:37,030
know that you can apply some of these vector operations

202
00:09:37,030 --> 00:09:42,110
to tensors, and you'll just get a tensor as the output.

203
00:09:42,110 --> 00:09:44,590
Another thing that might be important

204
00:09:44,590 --> 00:09:46,370
is you can clamp tensors.

205
00:09:46,370 --> 00:09:50,230
So if you, say, were worried about your outputs going too far

206
00:09:50,230 --> 00:09:54,350
in some direction, you can use .clamp and then some specific

207
00:09:54,350 --> 00:09:57,870
range to actually get a specifically clamped output.

208
00:09:57,870 --> 00:10:01,850
So we can see this plot here of that kind of transformation.

209
00:10:01,850 --> 00:10:04,410
And so yeah, there's lots of different transformations

210
00:10:04,410 --> 00:10:05,110
that we can do.

211
00:10:05,110 --> 00:10:07,235
I'm not going to be able to go through all of them,

212
00:10:07,235 --> 00:10:11,050
but just know that if you want to do some of operation

213
00:10:11,050 --> 00:10:14,250
or transformation on a tensor, there's probably some ability

214
00:10:14,250 --> 00:10:14,750
to do it.

215
00:10:14,750 --> 00:10:16,833
And you'll just have to look up the documentation.

216
00:10:20,330 --> 00:10:23,530
Moving on, we're going to start talking about how we can deal

217
00:10:23,530 --> 00:10:25,410
with multidimensional data.

218
00:10:25,410 --> 00:10:29,450
So we're going to be dealing with, frequently,

219
00:10:29,450 --> 00:10:31,610
some sort of data input that's in a batch.

220
00:10:31,610 --> 00:10:34,010
And I think we talked about batches in lectures today.

221
00:10:34,010 --> 00:10:37,810
But basically, we create a batch of some of inputs.

222
00:10:37,810 --> 00:10:40,530
And generally, what we do to represent

223
00:10:40,530 --> 00:10:43,610
that is we include the batch as the first dimension.

224
00:10:43,610 --> 00:10:46,670
And then this example is for an image.

225
00:10:46,670 --> 00:10:50,410
So you might do the channel of R, G, and B

226
00:10:50,410 --> 00:10:54,190
as the second dimension, and then the rest of your data,

227
00:10:54,190 --> 00:10:54,733
and so on.

228
00:10:54,733 --> 00:10:56,650
But in general I think the most important part

229
00:10:56,650 --> 00:11:03,110
there is that the batch index is generally the first dimension.

230
00:11:03,110 --> 00:11:06,750
Moving on, we can look at some of these tensors of higher

231
00:11:06,750 --> 00:11:10,150
dimensions and see how we can play with their shapes

232
00:11:10,150 --> 00:11:13,470
and see how this affects things.

233
00:11:13,470 --> 00:11:15,310
So if we create a random tensor, we're

234
00:11:15,310 --> 00:11:18,970
going to do this shape representing two images here.

235
00:11:18,970 --> 00:11:24,230
So we have two RGB images that are 5 by 4.

236
00:11:24,230 --> 00:11:27,370
And we can create this random--

237
00:11:27,370 --> 00:11:28,930
we'll just say random numbers.

238
00:11:28,930 --> 00:11:31,590
They're obviously not following RGB format,

239
00:11:31,590 --> 00:11:34,060
but that's OK for this example.

240
00:11:34,060 --> 00:11:35,810
One thing we can do is permute dimensions.

241
00:11:35,810 --> 00:11:38,910
So if we were interested in swapping the dimensions x and y

242
00:11:38,910 --> 00:11:42,243
within our image, we can use this permute command.

243
00:11:42,243 --> 00:11:43,910
And so what you do is you basically just

244
00:11:43,910 --> 00:11:46,530
order the dimensions how you want them to be ordered.

245
00:11:46,530 --> 00:11:48,790
So if you wanted to keep the batch size the same,

246
00:11:48,790 --> 00:11:51,790
and then the RGB dimension the same,

247
00:11:51,790 --> 00:11:55,790
we'd say 0 and 1 because those are indexed.

248
00:11:55,790 --> 00:11:59,350
And then if we wanted to swap, say, the x and y,

249
00:11:59,350 --> 00:12:02,650
we'd just swap the dimensions that they were originally

250
00:12:02,650 --> 00:12:06,850
in our permute statement.

251
00:12:06,850 --> 00:12:12,560
So if we run that, we see that we originally had a size of 2,

252
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:16,570
3, 4, 5, but in this case, we've swapped the 4 and the 5 with

253
00:12:16,570 --> 00:12:20,370
our .permute command.

254
00:12:20,370 --> 00:12:23,170
Another thing you might frequently do is reshaping.

255
00:12:23,170 --> 00:12:25,170
And so there's a few different ways to do this.

256
00:12:25,170 --> 00:12:27,210
The first is with .view.

257
00:12:27,210 --> 00:12:30,190
And this is generally the more efficient way.

258
00:12:30,190 --> 00:12:32,810
But there's also .reshape, and it has pretty much the same

259
00:12:32,810 --> 00:12:34,570
functionality.

260
00:12:34,570 --> 00:12:38,690
And basically, what we can do is we can input specific shapes

261
00:12:38,690 --> 00:12:39,350
that we want.

262
00:12:39,350 --> 00:12:42,090
So in this case, we're just keeping the batch size.

263
00:12:42,090 --> 00:12:43,753
And then the negative basically just

264
00:12:43,753 --> 00:12:46,170
means that we're going to have all of the rest of the data

265
00:12:46,170 --> 00:12:49,430
that we had included in one dimension.

266
00:12:49,430 --> 00:12:52,770
So we end up with a shape of just 2 and 60,

267
00:12:52,770 --> 00:12:57,090
because this negative 1 was just the rest of the data in each--

268
00:12:57,090 --> 00:12:59,630
the rest of those dimensions.

269
00:12:59,630 --> 00:13:01,830
Next thing to know is indexing.

270
00:13:01,830 --> 00:13:06,430
So PyTorch does indexing pretty much the same way as NumPy does.

271
00:13:06,430 --> 00:13:09,230
So if we wanted to, say, get something like the first row,

272
00:13:09,230 --> 00:13:11,610
we can just input a specific number.

273
00:13:11,610 --> 00:13:13,150
So that would be 0.

274
00:13:13,150 --> 00:13:15,970
And if we wanted to get the first column,

275
00:13:15,970 --> 00:13:18,110
we can use this colon to represent

276
00:13:18,110 --> 00:13:21,910
all of the rows and then the 0 to represent the first column.

277
00:13:21,910 --> 00:13:24,710
And so that's the second dimension.

278
00:13:24,710 --> 00:13:26,790
And then if we want to get the last thing,

279
00:13:26,790 --> 00:13:32,270
similar to other Python methods, you can just use negative 1.

280
00:13:32,270 --> 00:13:35,610
We can also use this slicing to assign values.

281
00:13:35,610 --> 00:13:39,710
So if we did wanted to assign the first column or, I guess,

282
00:13:39,710 --> 00:13:42,390
this second column to 0, we could

283
00:13:42,390 --> 00:13:44,110
do this using this assignment.

284
00:13:44,110 --> 00:13:47,210
The next thing we might want to do is join tensors together.

285
00:13:47,210 --> 00:13:52,550
So we might want to combine different maybe batches or just

286
00:13:52,550 --> 00:13:55,790
specific elements to create a larger tensor.

287
00:13:55,790 --> 00:13:57,870
And so we'll go through two different ways here.

288
00:13:57,870 --> 00:14:00,290
The first is just concatenating.

289
00:14:00,290 --> 00:14:02,610
So we use this torch.cat operator.

290
00:14:02,610 --> 00:14:08,170
Then we input our tensors in a list here.

291
00:14:08,170 --> 00:14:11,712
We also define a dimension that we want to concatenate along.

292
00:14:11,712 --> 00:14:13,170
And basically, what this will do is

293
00:14:13,170 --> 00:14:16,930
it will just add all of these input tensors

294
00:14:16,930 --> 00:14:18,950
together along that specific dimension.

295
00:14:18,950 --> 00:14:22,450
So we see here we have this tensor that's a 4 by 4.

296
00:14:22,450 --> 00:14:25,330
And if we concatenate the three tensors together

297
00:14:25,330 --> 00:14:28,030
along the first dimension dim equals 1.

298
00:14:28,030 --> 00:14:30,010
We're going to end up with a 4 by 12.

299
00:14:30,010 --> 00:14:33,890
And you can see how this works out with our second column

300
00:14:33,890 --> 00:14:37,490
and our tensor, all 0's.

301
00:14:37,490 --> 00:14:39,730
The next thing you can do is torch.stack.

302
00:14:39,730 --> 00:14:41,810
And instead of concatenating along one dimension,

303
00:14:41,810 --> 00:14:44,170
you're going to actually create a new dimension.

304
00:14:44,170 --> 00:14:48,010
So say if you had a few different inputs

305
00:14:48,010 --> 00:14:49,850
and you wanted to create a batch,

306
00:14:49,850 --> 00:14:53,530
you might do a stack along dimension equals 0,

307
00:14:53,530 --> 00:14:56,690
and you'll end up with an additional dimension

308
00:14:56,690 --> 00:14:58,290
at the start here.

309
00:14:58,290 --> 00:15:00,440
The next thing we can do is things

310
00:15:00,440 --> 00:15:02,462
like matrix multiplication.

311
00:15:02,462 --> 00:15:04,420
So there's a few different ways we can do this.

312
00:15:04,420 --> 00:15:08,240
All of these operations here do the same thing.

313
00:15:08,240 --> 00:15:11,100
I think using the at symbol is probably the easiest way,

314
00:15:11,100 --> 00:15:13,360
but you can pick whatever you would like.

315
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:15,400
And this just performs matrix multiplication,

316
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:17,280
as you would expect.

317
00:15:17,280 --> 00:15:22,000
And so, in this case, we made this tensor,

318
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:26,720
and we'll just apply matrix multiplication to it.

319
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:29,720
We can also compute element-wise products.

320
00:15:29,720 --> 00:15:32,440
So that will just be the multiplication symbol

321
00:15:32,440 --> 00:15:34,920
or this tensor.mul.

322
00:15:34,920 --> 00:15:37,260
And so that will do just an element-wise product.

323
00:15:37,260 --> 00:15:41,720
So if we had this tensor of 1, 2, 3, 4,

324
00:15:41,720 --> 00:15:46,060
we'll just end up with the squares element-wise product.

325
00:15:49,160 --> 00:15:53,480
Next thing to know is we can use some aggregators to find

326
00:15:53,480 --> 00:15:57,780
some different statistics or values within our tensors.

327
00:15:57,780 --> 00:16:03,700
So we can do things like sum, mean, max, min, stuff like that,

328
00:16:03,700 --> 00:16:06,700
and get the value along a specific dimension

329
00:16:06,700 --> 00:16:08,460
or along the whole tensor.

330
00:16:08,460 --> 00:16:13,875
So that concludes the overall tensor part of this.

331
00:16:13,875 --> 00:16:16,000
Definitely going a little slower than I was hoping,

332
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:20,820
but we'll try and get through as much as we can.

333
00:16:20,820 --> 00:16:24,323
So the next thing to talk about is the autograd of PyTorch.

334
00:16:24,323 --> 00:16:26,740
And autograd is one of the most important parts of PyTorch

335
00:16:26,740 --> 00:16:29,740
because it allows us to actually compute gradients and update

336
00:16:29,740 --> 00:16:32,820
our models so that they can actually learn and produce

337
00:16:32,820 --> 00:16:34,660
reasonable outputs.

338
00:16:34,660 --> 00:16:37,300
And so we can first use autograd to just calculate

339
00:16:37,300 --> 00:16:39,380
a simple gradient of a vector.

340
00:16:39,380 --> 00:16:42,100
So I think we saw a similar example here before,

341
00:16:42,100 --> 00:16:46,340
where we made this x, which is the values between 0 and 5.

342
00:16:46,340 --> 00:16:48,600
And then we applied an operator on this.

343
00:16:48,600 --> 00:16:52,500
So we're squaring it, and then taking the cosine here.

344
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:56,140
And we can find the derivative of this

345
00:16:56,140 --> 00:16:58,860
by using the autograd features of PyTorch.

346
00:16:58,860 --> 00:17:02,260
And so if we want to take a derivative,

347
00:17:02,260 --> 00:17:05,700
we actually need to have a scalar as an output.

348
00:17:05,700 --> 00:17:07,480
So we take the sum here, and then we

349
00:17:07,480 --> 00:17:12,359
can use this torch.autograd of that output with respect

350
00:17:12,359 --> 00:17:18,520
to x to get our actual derivative here, dy by dx.

351
00:17:18,520 --> 00:17:23,800
And so this is the backend, how you could specifically

352
00:17:23,800 --> 00:17:27,520
calculate one specific gradient or one derivative here.

353
00:17:27,520 --> 00:17:29,800
But when we're actually doing a model,

354
00:17:29,800 --> 00:17:33,320
PyTorch takes care of exactly which derivatives we need.

355
00:17:33,320 --> 00:17:36,080
And we don't have to do these really specific calculations

356
00:17:36,080 --> 00:17:39,613
of which differential we actually want.

357
00:17:39,613 --> 00:17:41,280
But we can see in this plot here that we

358
00:17:41,280 --> 00:17:47,040
have our x squared cosine, where we end up with a shorter period

359
00:17:47,040 --> 00:17:48,840
as we go to the right.

360
00:17:48,840 --> 00:17:51,680
And then we can see our gradient here,

361
00:17:51,680 --> 00:17:57,360
where it's the correct gradient of that value.

362
00:17:57,360 --> 00:18:00,040
We can do this with another function here.

363
00:18:00,040 --> 00:18:03,600
So if you have a polynomial and we want to compute the gradient,

364
00:18:03,600 --> 00:18:07,620
we can do this in the same way as we previously did.

365
00:18:07,620 --> 00:18:10,720
And we can run this, and we can see the result is the same.

366
00:18:10,720 --> 00:18:13,620
We end up with our plot, and we have our gradient

367
00:18:13,620 --> 00:18:17,180
of our plot as well.

368
00:18:17,180 --> 00:18:19,860
So these are this really specific way

369
00:18:19,860 --> 00:18:22,620
we can calculate this exact gradient.

370
00:18:22,620 --> 00:18:25,620
But PyTorch allows us to actually calculate

371
00:18:25,620 --> 00:18:27,703
all the gradients all together.

372
00:18:27,703 --> 00:18:29,620
And so we don't need to specifically calculate

373
00:18:29,620 --> 00:18:30,700
one gradient.

374
00:18:30,700 --> 00:18:33,460
Instead, we can just do a specific command,

375
00:18:33,460 --> 00:18:35,883
and we will get all the gradients that we want.

376
00:18:35,883 --> 00:18:37,300
And so that's what you'll actually

377
00:18:37,300 --> 00:18:39,560
be doing if you're running a PyTorch model.

378
00:18:39,560 --> 00:18:42,700
You won't need to calculate and store each different part

379
00:18:42,700 --> 00:18:45,020
individually.

380
00:18:45,020 --> 00:18:47,820
And so if we look at this, we have the same example as we had

381
00:18:47,820 --> 00:18:52,720
before, but instead of doing our autograd and using the brackets,

382
00:18:52,720 --> 00:18:55,340
as we saw before, we can just use this .backward,

383
00:18:55,340 --> 00:19:01,440
and that will store the gradient of each part within the tensor.

384
00:19:01,440 --> 00:19:04,360
And so if we just get the use .grad,

385
00:19:04,360 --> 00:19:07,680
we can actually get the specific gradient using the .backward

386
00:19:07,680 --> 00:19:08,880
command.

387
00:19:08,880 --> 00:19:12,240
So you'll never do the autograd.grad

388
00:19:12,240 --> 00:19:13,440
that we had up here.

389
00:19:13,440 --> 00:19:15,680
You'll always use the .backward to just calculate all

390
00:19:15,680 --> 00:19:17,680
the gradients that you need.

391
00:19:17,680 --> 00:19:19,645
So yeah, as I was mentioning, if you

392
00:19:19,645 --> 00:19:21,020
aren't going to use the gradient,

393
00:19:21,020 --> 00:19:22,540
you shouldn't calculate it.

394
00:19:22,540 --> 00:19:23,717
And we can use--

395
00:19:23,717 --> 00:19:25,300
I don't know if I have it listed here,

396
00:19:25,300 --> 00:19:31,160
but you would say torch.no_grad, and that would allow you to not

397
00:19:31,160 --> 00:19:35,760
use the gradient and speed up your computation.

398
00:19:35,760 --> 00:19:39,880
Oh yeah, here we go with torch.no_grad that would be what

399
00:19:39,880 --> 00:19:40,660
we would use.

400
00:19:43,640 --> 00:19:46,040
Next thing we're going to talk about is optimizers.

401
00:19:46,040 --> 00:19:48,807
So we figured out how we can calculate a gradient.

402
00:19:48,807 --> 00:19:50,640
But the next thing we need to do is actually

403
00:19:50,640 --> 00:19:55,280
use that gradient to optimize some function, some loss

404
00:19:55,280 --> 00:19:56,820
function, something like that.

405
00:19:56,820 --> 00:20:00,580
So this naive gradient descent-- obviously,

406
00:20:00,580 --> 00:20:02,660
it works for this scenario, but in a lot of cases

407
00:20:02,660 --> 00:20:06,820
we want to be more adaptive and speed up our training.

408
00:20:06,820 --> 00:20:11,060
So I think we saw stochastic gradient descent in lecture.

409
00:20:11,060 --> 00:20:12,600
But this is another method.

410
00:20:12,600 --> 00:20:14,700
We import this optimizer.

411
00:20:14,700 --> 00:20:16,780
And if we do optimizer.step it takes

412
00:20:16,780 --> 00:20:18,600
care of the gradient update.

413
00:20:18,600 --> 00:20:21,940
So it will use the gradient, and it will update

414
00:20:21,940 --> 00:20:23,160
the weights of our model.

415
00:20:25,780 --> 00:20:31,107
So if we run this, we see, again, our model minimizes.

416
00:20:31,107 --> 00:20:32,940
Maybe it got to the minimum a little faster,

417
00:20:32,940 --> 00:20:35,460
and then it's jumping around a little bit.

418
00:20:35,460 --> 00:20:37,920
This is just some little toy examples,

419
00:20:37,920 --> 00:20:42,220
so we're not too concerned exactly about what's happening.

420
00:20:42,220 --> 00:20:45,140
The last optimizer here we're going to look at is Adam.

421
00:20:45,140 --> 00:20:47,200
So Adam is a pretty common optimizer.

422
00:20:47,200 --> 00:20:50,580
And it adaptively does a lot of the steps.

423
00:20:50,580 --> 00:20:53,460
And it generally is the most efficient.

424
00:20:53,460 --> 00:20:55,740
So a lot of people use that in real scenarios

425
00:20:55,740 --> 00:20:57,660
as opposed to just naive gradient descent

426
00:20:57,660 --> 00:21:01,200
or stochastic gradient descent.

427
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:02,560
So we can run this one.

428
00:21:02,560 --> 00:21:07,360
It's the same structure as the SGD.

429
00:21:07,360 --> 00:21:10,000
But we see in this example, it's not quite doing

430
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:13,260
as well as some of the simpler ways, but in general,

431
00:21:13,260 --> 00:21:16,760
this is a pretty good method of optimizing.

432
00:21:16,760 --> 00:21:19,480
I think we can try and get through a quick bit

433
00:21:19,480 --> 00:21:21,800
of neural networks and how we can use

434
00:21:21,800 --> 00:21:25,080
PyTorch for neural networks.

435
00:21:25,080 --> 00:21:28,080
So obviously, that's a big part of deep learning

436
00:21:28,080 --> 00:21:29,840
is neural networks.

437
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:32,200
And so there's a lot of modules within PyTorch

438
00:21:32,200 --> 00:21:34,360
that we can use to create networks

439
00:21:34,360 --> 00:21:39,520
from just really simple MLPs to more complex models.

440
00:21:39,520 --> 00:21:43,600
So the first building block that we see here is the linear layer.

441
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:45,240
And so this is basically just going

442
00:21:45,240 --> 00:21:47,840
to do a transformation from one dimension

443
00:21:47,840 --> 00:21:53,640
to another dimension with a linear transformation.

444
00:21:53,640 --> 00:21:56,560
So we can generate that, and we can

445
00:21:56,560 --> 00:21:58,020
set the number of input features,

446
00:21:58,020 --> 00:22:04,140
the number of output features, along with a bias term as well.

447
00:22:04,140 --> 00:22:06,180
And so we could just run an input

448
00:22:06,180 --> 00:22:08,700
through our linear network.

449
00:22:08,700 --> 00:22:12,500
We put an input of size 3 and get an output of size 2.

450
00:22:12,500 --> 00:22:14,640
So yeah, then we can pass an input to that,

451
00:22:14,640 --> 00:22:17,340
and we see we had an input of 3.

452
00:22:17,340 --> 00:22:21,780
And then we end up with an output of size 2.

453
00:22:21,780 --> 00:22:23,080
We can also use a batch.

454
00:22:23,080 --> 00:22:27,420
So we have four inputs of size 3 here.

455
00:22:27,420 --> 00:22:30,640
And natively, it will just work to support that,

456
00:22:30,640 --> 00:22:32,680
and we get four outputs of size 2.

457
00:22:35,387 --> 00:22:37,220
If we can take a look at some of the weights

458
00:22:37,220 --> 00:22:41,860
and bias of this linear layer, we can just

459
00:22:41,860 --> 00:22:43,317
get the actual numbers.

460
00:22:43,317 --> 00:22:44,900
And so these are the numbers that it's

461
00:22:44,900 --> 00:22:48,300
going to multiply the inputs by to actually get an output.

462
00:22:48,300 --> 00:22:50,800
And the bias terms are added to the outputs.

463
00:22:53,620 --> 00:22:56,740
And you'll note the requires_grad=True on this

464
00:22:56,740 --> 00:23:00,480
tensor here, which shows that it's calculating the gradient

465
00:23:00,480 --> 00:23:01,340
as you go along.

466
00:23:05,040 --> 00:23:10,280
We can set specific things like the bias to 0 if we wanted to.

467
00:23:10,280 --> 00:23:13,960
And then we can see that our outputs will not

468
00:23:13,960 --> 00:23:16,460
add that bias term at the end.

469
00:23:19,760 --> 00:23:22,920
We can look at some of the parameters.

470
00:23:22,920 --> 00:23:25,760
Just if we do this net.name_parameters,

471
00:23:25,760 --> 00:23:29,400
and that will get the exact values within those weights

472
00:23:29,400 --> 00:23:31,200
as well.

473
00:23:31,200 --> 00:23:34,060
And we can see that we set it to 0, and the bias term is now 0.

474
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:42,260
We can look at a few different features,

475
00:23:42,260 --> 00:23:45,920
the shapes of our weights.

476
00:23:45,920 --> 00:23:49,460
We can load them.

477
00:23:49,460 --> 00:23:51,880
So yeah, we saved the weights, and then we

478
00:23:51,880 --> 00:23:54,617
can load the weights here.

479
00:23:54,617 --> 00:23:57,200
Sorry, I'm trying to go-- let's see if we can get to something

480
00:23:57,200 --> 00:24:00,340
a little more interesting.

481
00:24:00,340 --> 00:24:02,180
Maybe if we wanted to calculate a loss, so

482
00:24:02,180 --> 00:24:05,900
if we're trying to optimize this linear layer,

483
00:24:05,900 --> 00:24:08,140
and let's say we wanted to just--

484
00:24:08,140 --> 00:24:11,100
kind of a dumb example, but if we wanted to just always output

485
00:24:11,100 --> 00:24:14,820
1 comma 1, we could try and train our linear layer

486
00:24:14,820 --> 00:24:17,180
to always output 1 comma 1.

487
00:24:17,180 --> 00:24:20,900
So if we see here, first, we get an output.

488
00:24:20,900 --> 00:24:22,660
And it will calculate this loss, where

489
00:24:22,660 --> 00:24:27,620
we're doing squared loss here.

490
00:24:27,620 --> 00:24:29,380
And we could use the loss.backward

491
00:24:29,380 --> 00:24:30,900
to get the gradients.

492
00:24:30,900 --> 00:24:32,540
And then we could use those gradients

493
00:24:32,540 --> 00:24:35,680
to update our linear layer.

494
00:24:39,620 --> 00:24:42,980
So here, if we wanted to actually train this model

495
00:24:42,980 --> 00:24:46,340
to learn to always output 1 comma 1,

496
00:24:46,340 --> 00:24:47,600
we could do an update loop.

497
00:24:47,600 --> 00:24:52,020
So here we're just looping doing a batch input

498
00:24:52,020 --> 00:24:54,540
and then calculating the loss.

499
00:24:54,540 --> 00:24:57,460
We're getting the gradients here with loss.backward.

500
00:24:57,460 --> 00:24:59,250
And then we're just using gradient descent

501
00:24:59,250 --> 00:25:01,450
to update our parameters.

502
00:25:01,450 --> 00:25:05,450
And we can see if we run this, we

503
00:25:05,450 --> 00:25:07,550
should get a graph of what our loss is.

504
00:25:07,550 --> 00:25:09,390
So we end up with the following weights.

505
00:25:09,390 --> 00:25:12,450
You can see the weights within the linear layer just went to 0,

506
00:25:12,450 --> 00:25:14,490
and our bias went to 1.

507
00:25:14,490 --> 00:25:18,810
And so our loss went to near 0 because we've learned

508
00:25:18,810 --> 00:25:21,850
to always output 1 comma 1.

509
00:25:21,850 --> 00:25:25,290
I'll try and work through maybe one more part here,

510
00:25:25,290 --> 00:25:27,550
but feel free to go if you need to go.

511
00:25:30,250 --> 00:25:32,010
So the last thing I want to talk about

512
00:25:32,010 --> 00:25:34,450
is that we can create a more complex network using

